**Read:** [Production of Large Volume Organic Chemicals, BREF LVOC](../sources/Large-Volume-Organic-Chemicals-BREF.lingua#r_78ET6A4D7AX8HPQTYAMZMAG16B), the complete lower-olefins chapter including reaction, recovery, energy and residuals; [Organic Chemical Technology — Nanda Kishore, NPTEL](../sources/Organic-Chemical-Technology-Kishore.lingua#r_1ERYESBGNKCKMDY0NPHJKCVJG0), the full petrochemical/cracking and olefin teaching; [Refining of Mineral Oil and Gas, BREF REF](../sources/Mineral-Oil-and-Gas-Refining-BREF.lingua#r_3ZQ3XT8F2XTED0CF8KN79K9NP6), the entire relevant catalytic-cracking and connected product-system treatment.

Steam cracking and catalytic cracking employ different reaction environments, feed ranges and product objectives. For a simple ethane relation, C₂H₆→C₂H₄+H₂ is balanced, but real feeds form networks of products and deposits. Residence, temperature history, dilution, catalyst state where present, and rapid changes in conditions influence selectivity.

Olefin recovery requires a complete downstream separation/conditioning train. Compression, removal of contaminants, drying, fractionation and control of reactive species influence actual monomer quality and useful recovery. A reactor yield cannot substitute for the final product and energy account.

Trace furnace/heat supply, quench, coke/deposit formation, maintenance and returned material over time. Compare actual feeds and product slates rather than using a nominal cracking label as evidence of equivalent performance. Chemical and physical states at transfer points must remain explicit.

**Work:** reconstruct the entire lower-olefin process chapter and a contrasting conversion route with primary evidence. Independently close carbon/hydrogen and major heat/impurity accounts, explain the kinetic/transport and separation assumptions, and compare accepted monomer output through a full service interval. Identify which measurement would distinguish the proposed selectivity explanation from a separation or feed effect.

